WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.170
Thank you, Malcolm.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:15.070
There are four substantive pieces of business
that we will be discussing this morning, in

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:18.660
addition to our usual focus on operational
performance.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.341
The first is the outcome of the Spending Review,
which was announced on 25th November, five

00:00:23.341 --> 00:00:25.750
days after our last public Board meeting.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:33.190
The second is the publication of the mandate
to NHS England and the wider NHS, which has

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.620
been laid before Parliament this morning.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:42.110
The third is the principal item of business
here, which is the allocation of £560 billion

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:46.270
of public spending to the National Health
Service over the next five years.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:51.910
Fourthly, the work that is going on with our
partners, the other national leadership bodies

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:57.260
of the NHS, for the NHS planning guidance,
which we expect to be able to issue within

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:00.290
the next several days.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:04.420
Taking those in turn: on the outcome of the
Spending Review – as you will see in my

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:11.710
Chief Executive’s Report here, and as has
obviously been widely reported in the media

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:19.990
– judged against our request for a front-loaded
settlement, we have clearly achieved that:

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:25.830
£3.8 billion of real-terms growth for the
National Health Service next year.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:32.160
In effect, three fifths of the five year funding
will have been delivered in the first two

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.680
out of the five years.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:39.120
We have some very substantial funding pressures
in the system that have to be dealt with next

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:48.230
year – the pensions cost, the £1.8 billion
for the sustainability focus that was set

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:53.990
out yesterday – and those are all part,
frankly, of the reasons why the front-loaded

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:57.909
nature of the settlement was not only welcome,
it was necessary.

00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:04.170
At the Annual General Meeting, however, we
talked about four other tests in addition

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:08.640
to the front-loading, and I think it is worth
the Board just describing our assessment as

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.860
to where we have landed on those.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:16.250
In addition to front-loading, the second of
the tests was ensuring that there was a syncing-up

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:21.100
of the requests that were being made by the
Government to the National Health Service,

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:26.310
with the phasing of the extra funding year
by year over the five-year period.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:32.870
My judgement is that the mandate published
today, in terms of the affordability of the

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:39.390
asks for 2016/2017, corresponds to that with
a broad set of goals for 2020/2021.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:45.250
Obviously, there is a discussion about precisely
what the sequencing looks like over the rest

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:49.530
of the Parliament, but we effectively have
a U-shaped funding settlement, front-loading

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.430
and back loading.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:57.920
So as we think about logistically the roll-out
of some of the workforce and other developments,

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:03.270
including seven-day services and hospitals,
we will have that U-shaped funding settlement

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:04.599
in mind.

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:10.240
Thirdly, we made the point that we need a
realistic and deliverable set of efficiency

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:16.720
expectations, particularly through the traditional
provider-tariff mechanism, and I think we

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.270
have arrived at a situation where just slightly
less than half of the much-discussed £22

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:25.910
billion, slightly under £11 billion, will
have to come from that source.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:30.330
There is also wider action that the Department
of Health, government and national leadership

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:37.270
bodies will need to take, and in fact we now
have a clear and detailed description of those

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:43.520
efficiencies, and the Government will be making
announcements on elements of those shortly.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:49.290
However, there were two other elements to
the five tests that were outside the immediate

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:53.230
bailiwick of the National Health Service,
one of those around prevention and public

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:54.440
health.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:04:03.080
There were continuing funding cuts in local
authority finance, public health services,

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:05.840
on the back of the Spending Review.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:13.260
My judgement is that we need to see concerted
action from Government, both nationally and

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:18.330
in terms of new powers for local government
– the manifesto of the new Childhood Obesity

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:22.830
Strategy in the new year, for example – in
order to be able to say that that test would

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:24.569
have been met.

00:04:24.569 --> 00:04:31.960
On the fifth of our five, we made the point
that the level of demand facing the NHS was

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:36.919
obviously a function of the availability of
social care.

00:04:36.919 --> 00:04:43.229
There were, as I say in my report, some welcome
moves to hypothecate new funding streams through

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:50.099
the precept, through a funded expansion of
Better Care Fund, and other mechanisms.

00:04:50.099 --> 00:04:54.550
Nevertheless, even with that, there will still
be – as the Local Government Association

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:59.289
and the Association of Directors of Adult
Social Services have pointed out – a widening

00:04:59.289 --> 00:05:04.689
gap between the availability of funding and
the need for social care.

00:05:04.689 --> 00:05:10.409
If that is not addressed over the remainder
of the period through 2020, then that will

00:05:10.409 --> 00:05:16.349
result in extra demand over and above that
which was modelled into the Five Year Forward

00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:17.349
View.

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:21.139
That is just a statement of fact; it is not
a controversial statement, I do not think,

00:05:21.139 --> 00:05:26.759
but I think it is important that as a Board
we understand how we have landed on those

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:29.869
five tests.

00:05:29.869 --> 00:05:32.500
Allocations is obviously what we are going
to spend the bulk of the time talking about

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:33.500
today.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:34.759
Paul will walk us through that.

00:05:34.759 --> 00:05:39.080
I would simply underline the fact that we
are seeking to do three things, I think, with

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:42.240
the allocation recommendations before the
Board this morning.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:47.910
First, we want to provide the wherewithal
to stabilise performance and fund the implementation

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:53.900
of the Forward View, and we are proposing
doing that with the creation of a Sustainability

00:05:53.900 --> 00:06:00.229
and Transformation Fund to the tune of £2.1
billion next year, of which £1.8 billion

00:06:00.229 --> 00:06:06.020
would principally be to do with the financial
pressures in the National Health Service,

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:10.749
the sustainability element, if you like; £300
million – £340 million, in fact – to

00:06:10.749 --> 00:06:17.990
continue with vanguards and expand some of
our work on implementing the Forward View

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:19.309
in 2016/2017.

00:06:19.309 --> 00:06:25.279
More importantly, the Transformation Fund
would grow from £2.1 billion next year to

00:06:25.279 --> 00:06:32.259
£2.9 billion the year after, reaching £3.4
billion by 2020/2021, and an increasing share

00:06:32.259 --> 00:06:37.979
of that expanding Transformation Fund would
be available for the implementation of the

00:06:37.979 --> 00:06:42.999
new care models, the parity of esteem on mental
health, the technology and other investments

00:06:42.999 --> 00:06:44.699
that we want to see.

00:06:44.699 --> 00:06:49.289
So the first theme is stabilising performance
and funding the Forward View.

00:06:49.289 --> 00:06:57.409
The second theme of our recommendations is
ensuring that we have reasonable funding locally,

00:06:57.409 --> 00:07:02.039
with our thumb on the scale, particularly
for primary care, and also ensuring that every

00:07:02.039 --> 00:07:07.029
CCG is getting real-terms growth, while recognising
that there will continue to be funding pressures

00:07:07.029 --> 00:07:11.819
in specialised care; particularly next year,
as Paul will establish, for some of the new

00:07:11.819 --> 00:07:13.770
hepatitis C drugs.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:17.250
That is something not in our discretion that
we cannot ignore.

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:23.339
Then thirdly, continuing the strong push that
this Board has made since its creation, ensuring

00:07:23.339 --> 00:07:28.240
that we use the allocations process to contribute
to inequalities reduction.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:35.869
You will recall that, this time last year,
we managed to reduce from 34 to 17 the number

00:07:35.869 --> 00:07:42.219
of CCGs who were more than 5% below their
fair shares, with the commitment to seek to

00:07:42.219 --> 00:07:46.240
deal with the remaining 17 this year.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:54.479
The recommendation before you would get every
CCG no more than 5% below its fair share;

00:07:54.479 --> 00:07:58.729
not just for CCG-commissioned services, but
for the first time also taking into account

00:07:58.729 --> 00:08:06.029
the total healthcare usage of their population,
so CCG services but also primary care and

00:08:06.029 --> 00:08:07.360
specialised services.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:13.059
Then over and above that, for the first time
putting in an explicit inequalities adjustment

00:08:13.059 --> 00:08:18.849
for specialised commissioning spend, and making
substantial progress in the fairness of allocations

00:08:18.849 --> 00:08:22.369
for primary care services that Paul will describe.

00:08:22.369 --> 00:08:29.559
In a nutshell, stabilising performance, investing
in the Forward View, local funding that enables

00:08:29.559 --> 00:08:34.610
people to make their plans work, and action
to cut inequalities.

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:37.899
Those are the recommendations before you.

00:08:37.899 --> 00:08:45.620
I will also just mention in this context that,
with the leadership of Paul and colleagues,

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:49.870
we are continuing progress in our devolution
discussions across the country.

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:54.980
As part of this package, you will see we are
recommending that we reach agreement with

00:08:54.980 --> 00:09:02.770
Greater Manchester on a carved-out, ringfenced
settlement for them.

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:08.800
In addition, together with the Chancellor,
the Mayor of London and the Health Secretary,

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:15.780
I launched five integration pilots across
London earlier in the week on Tuesday, and

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:22.120
have met with my colleagues, the leaders of
councils from the North East, to talk about

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:31.020
the health agenda that they are keen to pursue
and we are wanting to work with them on.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:35.390
I think we also need to recognise that this
Board established a clear set of criteria

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:45.640
that we thought should be applied to genuine
efforts to decentralise or devolve funding,

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:48.200
and we need to make sure that those are rigorously
applied.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:53.520
Frankly, where there are problems about that,
we should call a spade a spade, as we have

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:59.660
done recently – in fact, within the last
week – in respect of Kernow CCG in Cornwall,

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:01.640
where we have had to issue legal directions.

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:07.240
So the prospect that they would be in a position
to take on substantial extra delegated, devolved

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:12.270
powers, I think they have a lot of work to
do before we could imagine that that would

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:13.270
be real.

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:19.240
You will see from the Other Activities section
of my report that, as usual, there is a range

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:25.300
of front line engagement: joint work with
NHS Improvement, including two face-to-face

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:31.050
meetings of all Trust and CCG Chief Executives,
that Jim Mackey and I held a couple of Fridays

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:33.690
ago in Leeds and London, which was very important.

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:39.510
In fact, we held a joint NHS England/NHS Improvement
meeting of our regional directors to talk

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:45.550
about place-based challenges across the country
yesterday afternoon.

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:51.090
Any questions, of course, but I think that
is to some extent setting the table for the

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:52.850
substance of the discussions we are about
to have.

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:53.850
Thanks, Simon.

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:59.370
Let me extend his invitation to the Board
for any comments, comments, on the Chief Executive’s

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:01.070
Report.

